        // the intended recipient via the actor being msg.sender for token assignment. Since C uses
        // tokenBalance[msg.sender], we need the BankHelper to call c.airDrop while its own msg.sender is actor.
        // To accomplish this, we have BankHelper.callAirDropAs(actor, address(c)) which makes an external
        // call to c.airDrop() with BankHelper.msg.sender == actor (because BankHelper will externally call c
        // using the provided actor via vm.prank). However, Foundry's vm.prank sets msg.sender for the next call
        // from the test, not inside the helper. Simpler: we have the helper expose a function callAirDropAs(address caller, C target)
        // that performs target.airDrop() but the helper itself will be the msg.sender; to make tokenBalance keyed
        // by the intended actor, the helper implements supportsToken() and also proxies tokenBalance update by
        // using delegatecall pattern is not available. Instead, we can exploit that C checks Bank(msg.sender).supportsToken()
        // and then uses tokenBalance[msg.sender] — so to have tokenBalance updated for `actor`, we need msg.sender == actor.
        // Therefore, we perform vm.prank to set msg.sender to actor and call c.airDrop() directly; but then c will call Bank(msg.sender).supportsToken()
        // which expects that msg.sender is a Bank contract. So we must instead have actor be the BankHelper contract address in the fuzz domain.
